1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game. From beginners to the World Champion, everyone plays it. The bishop targets the f7-pawn and controls d5, rarely allowing Black counterplay in the center.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king into safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black attacks the e4-pawn and prepares to castle short. We'll play the striking move d4! We are using the fact that we have an extra starting move, and our king has already castled. Black has to "waste" a move to capture the d4-pawn. For the price of the d-pawn, we will get more space in the center and will be able to develop faster.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {After 5...exd4, we have officially entered Max Lange Attack territory. With e5, we strike in the center right away! Black already has a big problem on their hands. Finding the right move isn't easy. On the other hand, our position is easy to navigate.} 6.e5 6...d5 {6...d5 is Black's best attempt to fight off our attack. However, d5 is not the easiest move to find and leads to complicated positions. We are now reaching forcing variations with multiple captures, the first of which is on f6. We take the knight.} 7.exf6 7...dxc4 {A forced move, however, lots of lower-rated players will be uneasy with us taking the g7 pawn. We'll grab it and hit the rook!} 8.fxg7 8...Rg8 {The only available square for Black's rook is g8. Let's give check! We activate our pieces!} 9.Re1+ 9...Be6 {Black blocks the e-file with the light-squared bishop. Black dreams about having time to develop their queen and castle long. With Bg5, we continue to pose problems. Sometimes our idea can be to put the g5-bishop on f6, reinforcing the g7-pawn and paralyzing the g8-rook.} 10.Bg5 10...Be7 {Black protects their queen from attack and tries to remain solid. We'll exchange bishops. Now it will be challenging for Black to defend the dark squares.} 11.Bxe7 {We have transposed to the 9...Be6 line.} 11...Qxe7 {With 11...Qe7, Black gives the d4-pawn but prepares to take control over the d-file. We'll take on d4 with our knight.} 12.Nxd4 12...Rd8 {This is the best response for Black. Black attacks and pins our d4-knight but does not allow tricky jumps.} 13.c3 {A simple yet effective way to defend our knight. In the next few moves we will develop our queen and remaining knight, and if Black takes on d4, we'll recapture with the c3-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Max Lange Attack / Study 1: Main Lines After 5...exd4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/5d1c9e86-5a49-4c8d-bc5c-8cccb651daf3/studies/46560be6-2ddb-4058-ac7e-d138bdfa6bbe/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game. From beginners to the World Champion, everyone plays it. The bishop targets the f7-pawn and controls d5, rarely allowing Black counterplay in the center.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king into safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black attacks the e4-pawn and prepares to castle short. We'll play the striking move d4! We are using the fact that we have an extra starting move, and our king has already castled. Black has to "waste" a move to capture the d4-pawn. For the price of the d-pawn, we will get more space in the center and will be able to develop faster.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {After 5...exd4, we have officially entered Max Lange Attack territory. With e5, we strike in the center right away! Black already has a big problem on their hands. Finding the right move isn't easy. On the other hand, our position is easy to navigate.} 6.e5 6...d5 {6...d5 is Black's best attempt to fight off our attack. However, d5 is not the easiest move to find and leads to complicated positions. We are now reaching forcing variations with multiple captures, the first of which is on f6. We take the knight.} 7.exf6 7...dxc4 {A forced move, however, lots of lower-rated players will be uneasy with us taking the g7 pawn. We'll grab it and hit the rook!} 8.fxg7 8...Rg8 {The only available square for Black's rook is g8. Let's give check! We activate our pieces!} 9.Re1+ 9...Be6 {Black blocks the e-file with the light-squared bishop. Black dreams about having time to develop their queen and castle long. With Bg5, we continue to pose problems. Sometimes our idea can be to put the g5-bishop on f6, reinforcing the g7-pawn and paralyzing the g8-rook.} 10.Bg5 10...Be7 {Black protects their queen from attack and tries to remain solid. We'll exchange bishops. Now it will be challenging for Black to defend the dark squares.} 11.Bxe7 {We have transposed to the 9...Be6 line.} 11...Kxe7 {Black wants to retain the defense of the d4-pawn. We'll play very dynamically. Instead of developing the remaining pieces, we lift our Rook and threaten to capture the d4-pawn.} 12.Re4 12...d3 {This pawn advance is another way for Black to defend the d-pawn. By playing d3, Black weakened the c4-pawn. We are threatening to capture it after we play Nd2.} 13.Nbd2 13...dxc2 {This move doesn't really help Black much.} 14.Qxc2 {A position one can truly dream of. The c4 pawn is about to fall, and we are only one move away from our last piece entering the game. Black's king will be stuck in the center for a long time. Our chances are great in this position!} *

