h the light-squared bishop. Black dreams about having time to develop their queen and castle long. With Bg5, we continue to pose problems. Sometimes our idea can be to put the g5-bishop on f6, reinforcing the g7-pawn and paralyzing the g8-rook.} 10.Bg5 10...Qd5 {Simply put, this is a blunder. Black did not want to retreat the c5-bishop, but their queen is now a target. Let's attack it with Nc3! Black cannot take the c3-knight as the queen is unprotected because of the pinned e6-bishop.} 11.Nc3 11...Qf5 {Black is not happy to move the queen again, but there is no other option. The f5 square is the only sensible square. We'll play Ne4, threatening a lethal fork on f6.} 12.Ne4 12...Rxg7 {If the rook takes the pawn...} 13.Nh4 {That's all she wrote! Black loses the queen after Nf6+ on the next move.} *

