[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/5d1c9e86-5a49-4c8d-bc5c-8cccb651daf3/studies/f5ffaa04-66e7-493f-8ace-9c97e9249c22/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5 pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game. From beginners to the World Champion, everyone plays it. The bishop targets the f7-pawn and controls d5, rarely allowing Black counterplay in the center.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king into safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black attacks the e4-pawn and prepares to castle short. We'll play the striking move d4! We are using the fact that we have an extra starting move, and our king has already castled. Black has to "waste" a move to capture the d4-pawn. For the price of the d-pawn, we will get more space in the center and will be able to develop faster.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {After 5...exd4, we have officially entered Max Lange Attack territory. With e5, we strike in the center right away! Black already has a big problem on their hands. Finding the right move isn't easy. On the other hand, our position is easy to navigate.} 6.e5 6...Ng8 {Black decides it is best to put their knight back in the stable. Unfortunately for Black, that's rarely a good way to continue. Falling behind on piece development usually leads to easy wins. We'll keep up the pressure and attack f7 by playing Ng5.} 7.Ng5 7...Nh6 {The only sensible way to defend the f7-pawn.} 8.Re1 {A simple yet deadly move. We are not allowing Black to move the d-pawn because their king is stuck on the e-file, and we would just take the pawn, with a discovered check from the rook. On the other hand, if Black decides to castle, Black will be met by a deadly Qd3 surprise.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Max Lange Attack / Study 2: Sidelines After 5...exd4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/5d1c9e86-5a49-4c8d-bc5c-8cccb651daf3/studies/f5ffaa04-66e7-493f-8ace-9c97e9249c22/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5 pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game. From beginners to the World Champion, everyone plays it. The bishop targets the f7-pawn and controls d5, rarely allowing Black counterplay in the center.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king into safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black attacks the e4-pawn and prepares to castle short. We'll play the striking move d4! We are using the fact that we have an extra starting move, and our king has already castled. Black has to "waste" a move to capture the d4-pawn. For the price of the d-pawn, we will get more space in the center and will be able to develop faster.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {After 5...exd4, we have officially entered Max Lange Attack territory. With e5, we strike in the center right away! Black already has a big problem on their hands. Finding the right move isn't easy. On the other hand, our position is easy to navigate.} 6.e5 6...Ng4 {Black's attacking the e5-pawn, but the g4-knight is a little misplaced.} 7.Bf4 {We are protecting the e5-pawn and are getting ready to bring more pieces into the game. Some of our next moves can be Re1, Nd2, and h3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Max Lange Attack / Study 2: Sidelines After 5...exd4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/5d1c9e86-5a49-4c8d-bc5c-8cccb651daf3/studies/f5ffaa04-66e7-493f-8ace-9c97e9249c22/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5 pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game. From beginners to the World Champion, everyone plays it. The bishop targets the f7-pawn and controls d5, rarely allowing Black counterplay in the center.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king into safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black attacks the e4-pawn and prepares to castle short. We'll play the striking move d4! We are using the fact that we have an extra starting move, and our king has already castled. Black has to "waste" a move to capture the d4-pawn. For the price of the d-pawn, we will get more space in the center and will be able to develop faster.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {After 5...exd4, we have officially entered Max Lange Attack territory. With e5, we strike in the center right away! Black already has a big problem on their hands. Finding the right move isn't easy. On the other hand, our position is easy to navigate.} 6.e5 6...Ne4 {A game-losing blunder. We simply play Qe2, and Black will not be able to defend the e4-knight. Often you will see this mistake. Moves similar to this one are often the main continuation, followed by reinforcement from pawns.} 7.Qe2 7...d5 {A last attempt to save the e4-knight.} 8.exd6 {But we can take en passant and the knight is still hanging. We will continue with Bg5 and Nbd2, putting maximum pressure on Black's position.} *

