[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 1: Black Avoids The Deutz (6...exd4) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b298cbdc-38ff-41b6-8c20-4ffeec9a14dc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5 pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in the majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...exd4 {Taking on d4 with the pawn is a big mistake. Black has transposed to a worse version of the Max Lange Gambit. Also, in a way, it defeats the purpose of Black's last move. The whole idea of giving the bishop pair was to maintain the structure. We'll play energetically, and push forward with e5.} 7.e5 7...d5 {As we have seen in previous chapters, d5 is Black's best try in this type of position. After this move, we enter a forced sequence. We will capture on g7 and bring our pieces into the game.} 8.exf6 8...dxc4 {The only move. We'll grab the g7-pawn with an attack on the h8-rook. Black has to react, giving us time to continue energetically.} 9.fxg7 9...Rg8 {The only available square for Black's rook is g8. We'll play Re1+, and we can clearly see why this line is so bad for Black. Without the dark-squared bishop, Black does not have the option of retreating it to e7.} 10.Re1+ 10...Be6 {Black blocks the e-file with the light-squared bishop. Black dreams of having time to develop the queen and castle long. Now 11. Qh5 is the crucial move that secures our advantage. We threaten to take on e6 because the f7-pawn is pinned. Also, we have a very strong threat of Bg5 attacking the queen and cutting the black king off from the queenside.} 11.Qh5 11...Qf6 {With Qf6, Black protects the e6-bishop but also has an idea to take on g7 and threaten checkmate, or to play Qg6 to try and exchange queens. We'll play Bg5, a developing move that makes sure we are on top. Black's dreams of castling long are only that - dreams. Black must handle problems on the kingside.} 12.Bg5 12...Qg6 {The only sensible move for Black. A similar position could occur if instead of 12...Qg6, Black played 12...Qf5 13.g4 Qg6. The best way for us to continue is to enter an endgame. We have a strong, passed g7-pawn and well-positioned pieces.} 13.Qxg6 13...hxg6 {The only move.} 14.Bf6 {Our bishop is protecting the g7-pawn and still cuts the black king off from castling queen side. In the next few moves, we will develop the rest of our queen side pieces. We claim that Black's c4- and d4- pawns are weaknesses rather than strengths.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 1: Black Avoids The Deutz (6...exd4) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b298cbdc-38ff-41b6-8c20-4ffeec9a14dc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5 pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in the majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...exd4 {Taking on d4 with the pawn is a big mistake. Black has transposed to a worse version of the Max Lange Gambit. Also, in a way, it defeats the purpose of Black's last move. The whole idea of giving the bishop pair was to maintain the structure. We'll play energetically, and push forward with e5.} 7.e5 7...d5 {As we have seen in previous chapters, d5 is Black's best try in this type of position. After this move, we enter a forced sequence. We will capture on g7 and bring our pieces into the game.} 8.exf6 8...dxc4 {The only move. We'll grab the g7-pawn with an attack on the h8-rook. Black has to react, giving us time to continue energetically.} 9.fxg7 9...Rg8 {The only available square for Black's rook is g8. We'll play Re1+, and we can clearly see why this line is so bad for Black. Without the dark-squared bishop, Black does not have the option of retreating it to e7.} 10.Re1+ 10...Be6 {Black blocks the e-file with the light-squared bishop. Black dreams of having time to develop the queen and castle long. Now 11. Qh5 is the crucial move that secures our advantage. We threaten to take on e6 because the f7-pawn is pinned. Also, we have a very strong threat of Bg5 attacking the queen and cutting the black king off from the queenside.} 11.Qh5 11...Qf6 {With Qf6, Black protects the e6-bishop but also has an idea to take on g7 and threaten checkmate, or to play Qg6 to try and exchange queens. We'll play Bg5, a developing move that makes sure we are on top. Black's dreams of castling long are only that - dreams. Black must handle problems on the kingside.} 12.Bg5 12...Qxg7 {Eliminating the pawn, but forgetting that the queen was needed to protect the e6-bishop.} 13.Rxe6+ {Black is down a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...d5 {d5 is the best and the most energetic response for Black. However, this move is very sharp and not easy to find in a real game. Black's idea is to open up the c8-h3 diagonal. Besides that, Black gains an extra move to handle the position. We'll take the d-pawn.} 10.exd5 10...gxh4 {Black takes our bishop. We'll take on e5, after which both black knights are en prise.} 11.fxe5 11...Ng4 {Black can try to create a counterattack, but we'll capture on d4 and prevent the threat of Ne3.} 12.Qxd4 12...Qg5 {The queen protects the g4-knight. Also, Black would like to play Rg8 and try to arrange checkmate on g2.} 13.e6 {But with e6, we threaten both the h8-rook and a capture on f7. We stand very well, with a huge attack on the black king.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...d5 {d5 is the best and the most energetic response for Black. However, this move is very sharp and not easy to find in a real game. Black's idea is to open up the c8-h3 diagonal. Besides that, Black gains an extra move to handle the position. We'll take the d-pawn.} 10.exd5 10...Nxd5 {Nxd5 is the simplest way to continue. At the same time, it is very decent considering Black's position. Black's f6-knight is no longer a target and the g5-pawn is now protected by the queen. We'll take on g5, opening the f-file.} 11.fxg5 11...hxg5 {Black has to recapture. Now, by exchanging on d5, we force Black's queen to move away from the defense of the g5-pawn.} 12.Bxd5 12...Qxd5 {Black has to recapture.} 13.Bxg5 {We want to continue here by developing the knight. Our goal should be to get the knight to f6. Also, we want to develop our queen to d2 and bring the a1-rook into the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...gxh4 {Most often, we will see this move played by a lower-rated player. Yes, Black did capture our bishop, but it is likely that our opponent did not calculate the line very well. We'll take on e5. Both knights are now en prise, and f7 is under huge pressure.} 10.fxe5 10...d5 {Black's last chance to stay in the game is to go for a counterattack. There are a few good options for us in this position, but Qxd4 is the simplest. We enter an endgame where we will have a clear advantage.} 11.Qxd4 11...dxc4 {Black has to capture our bishop. Otherwise, we would capture on d5 with the e-pawn. We'll exchange queens.} 12.Qxd8+ 12...Kxd8 {The only move.} 13.exf6 {After all is said and done, we have an endgame where White is clearly better. While the material is balanced, Black's pawn structure is left in ruins. With doubled c- and h-pawns there isn't much hope. Our plan in this position is to develop the knight and attack the weak pawns with our rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...d5 {d5 is the best and the most energetic response for Black. However, this move is very sharp and not easy to find in a real game. Black's idea is to open up the c8-h3 diagonal. Besides that, Black gains an extra move to handle the position. We'll take the d-pawn.} 10.exd5 10...Bg4 {Bg4 is the most commonly played move in master games. Black wants to develop the bishop and gain a tempo on our queen. We'll play Qe1, threatening to take the e5-pawn.} 11.Qe1 11...Nxc2 {The most critical line. Black forces us to show why we are giving up the a1-rook. We'll take on e5 with check.} 12.Qxe5+ 12...Qe7 {Since the black king is in trouble, it makes sense for Black to offer the trade of queens. But we can safely exchange, because the knight on f6 is going to turn out to be unfortunately placed.} 13.Qxe7+ 13...Kxe7 {Black has to capture back.} 14.fxg5 {This move solves all of our problems and shows we are better in this position. If Black continued by taking the a1-rook, we would be able to capture the f6-knight with a check and play our knight to a3. Black's a1-knight is trapped and we will pick it up on the next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...d5 {d5 is the best and the most energetic response for Black. However, this move is very sharp and not easy to find in a real game. Black's idea is to open up the c8-h3 diagonal. Besides that, Black gains an extra move to handle the position. We'll take the d-pawn.} 10.exd5 10...gxh4 {Black takes our bishop. We'll take on e5, after which both black knights are en prise.} 11.fxe5 11...Nxd5 {Besides playing Ng4, this is the only other move that makes sense. In fact, this is the better move because it also captures the d-pawn and centralizes the knight. We'll capture on d4.} 12.Qxd4 12...Be6 {Black protects the d5-knight and prepares to castle long.} 13.Qf2 {We'll play Qf2, putting pressure on f7. In this position, we should continue by exchanging our bishop for the knight, then playing Nc3 and Rad1. Strategically, we would like to enter an endgame because Black has a lot of weak pawns. It is easy to imagine that if there were no pieces on the board, our king could simply capture the h4 and h6 pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...gxf4 {...gxf4 is the second most popular move at lower levels. Most often, players who have played this move feared falling into a trap after taking the h4-bishop. With this move, Black hopes to keep the position solid. We'll play the stunning Rxf4, sacrificing our rook to blow the position open!} 10.Rxf4 10...exf4 {...exf4 is the most obvious continuation.} 11.Qxd4 {Black can not defend the f6-knight. Black's position is simply lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Deutz Gambit / Study 2: The Deutz Gambit (Part 1) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/ca43e271-5dd9-4b7e-9582-9eaf665c79ba/studies/b65040e1-d805-4183-94d3-af972c5b2c70/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5-pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {By far the most common reply for Black. Black is defending the attacked e5-pawn and developing the knight at the same time. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Besides 3...Nf6, the main way to continue. Black wants to maintain the control over the d4 square. We want to castle as soon as possible! Firstly, we want to bring our king to safety and then play aggressively.} 4.O-O 4...Nf6 {Again, the most popular continuation. Black's attacking the e4-pawn and getting ready to castle short. And just like in the previous chapter, we strike in the center with many of the same ideas!} 5.d4 5...Bxd4 {5... Bxd4 is the most solid way to continue for Black. Also, it has been played in majority of master games. Black's idea is to keep the center structure intact. But taking with the bishop on d4 also has its downsides. We can capture it immediately and seize the bishop pair.} 6.Nxd4 6...Nxd4 {Nxd4 is Black's best continuation. Black's structure remains solid. All in all, Black forces us to show why we have sacrificed the d-pawn. By sacrificing the d-pawn, we are able to develop our bishop immediately. Our idea is to put pressure on the f6-knight. In most lines, we will follow up by playing f4 and opening up the f-file.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black is forcing us to declare on which diagonal we will keep our bishop. There is also an idea to follow up ...h6 with ...g5 which has recently been very popular in all kinds of 1. e4 e5 positions. We'll drop back to h4. Our idea is still the same: to keep the pressure on the f6-knight and push through with f4.} 8.Bh4 8...g5 {g5 is the critical move in the position. Black wants to push our bishop back to g3, and after d6 claim our bishop is semi-useless because it is hitting a pawn chain. Also, Black has a clamp over the f4 square, stopping our main idea. But we'll play f4 anyway! It will come as a shock because the f4 square is covered twice.} 9.f4 9...gxf4 {...gxf4 is the second most popular move at lower levels. Most often, players who have played this move feared falling into a trap after taking the h4-bishop. With this move, Black hopes to keep the position solid. We'll play the stunning Rxf4, sacrificing our rook to blow the position open!} 10.Rxf4 10...Ne2+ {Since Black will lose the knight anyway, Black argues that our queen is worse on e2 than it would be on d4.} 11.Qxe2 11...exf4 {The only move.} 12.e5 {Once again, e5 is the final blow! Black cannot defend the f6 Knight. After the next move, we will have two pieces for a rook. Our bishops are very active, and the game will not last long.} *

