[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {Black takes the free pawn and hopes to get to play d5 on the next move in order to close the diagonal for our bishop and protect their king in the center. We'll take on e5, opening up the d-file for the queen. The d5-square is very attractive, since the queen can threaten both f7 and e4 at the same time from there.} 5.dxe5 5...d5 {Black opens up the bishop and protects the knight. We'll just take on d5, reviving the threat to the knight.} 6.Bxd5 6...Nc5 {Black moves the knight away and intends to put it on e6 to block the diagonal for our bishop. We'll trade our d5-bishop for the knight in order to completely destroy Black's queenside pawn structure.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black has to recapture. Otherwise, they have lost a whole piece. White exchanges the queens and keeps the black king in the center. When our opponent's pawn structure is bad, the endgame usually suits us.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Kxd8 {Black has to take.} 9.Bg5+ {We develop the bishop with check - if Black blocks it with Be7, we'll trade and continue by developing our knight and castling queenside in order to connect the rooks and capitalize on our better pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {Black takes the free pawn and hopes to get to play d5 on the next move in order to close the diagonal for our bishop and protect their king in the center. We'll take on e5, opening up the d-file for the queen. The d5-square is very attractive, since the queen can threaten both f7 and e4 at the same time from there.} 5.dxe5 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop, threatening our e5-pawn. We'll punish Black's slow development by winning an important pawn with the temporary piece sacrifice Bxf7+!} 6.Bxf7+ 6...Kxf7 {Black has no better option than to go along with our plan. We'll fork the king and knight with Qd5+.} 7.Qd5+ 7...Be6 {Black blocks the check and develops the bishop.} 8.Qxe4 {White regains the sacrificed piece with interest. If Black doesn't stop it, we are threatening Ng5+. In the next few moves, White should castle and finish their queenside development. After that, it will be time to start harassing the exposed black king.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...Nxd4 {Black captures the d4-pawn and tries to keep the central files closed, but unfortunately for them, the e5-pawn is now unprotected. With the neat Bxf7+, we'll immediately punish Black for leaving the defense of e5 by winning an important pawn and misplacing the black king.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Kxf7 {Black has no better option than to go along with White's temporary bishop sacrifice. We'll take on e5 with check, eliminating the defender of the d4-knight.} 6.Nxe5+ 6...Kg8 {Black runs back behind their pawns, where the king should be safe.} 7.Qxd4 {White regains the piece with interest. In the future, White will castle, finish their queenside development and then start harassing the weak black king.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {Black takes the free pawn and hopes to get to play d5 on the next move in order to close the diagonal for our bishop and protect their king in the center. We'll take on e5, opening up the d-file for the queen. The d5-square is very attractive, since the queen can threaten both f7 and e4 at the same time from there.} 5.dxe5 5...Nc5 {Black pulls the knight back and intends to put it on e6 to block the white bishop. We'll simply castle.} 6.O-O 6...Be7 {Black develops the bishop and gets ready to castle. We'll develop the b1-knight towards the center.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {Black brings their king into safety.} 8.Nd5 {We centralize our knight, eyeing c7, which is only protected by the queen. White should focus on finishing their development and then trying to attack the barely protected black king.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d6 {Black fortifies the center and opens up the bishop. A small downside of the move is that the dark-squared bishop is blocked. We'll castle our king into safety.} 5.O-O 5...exd4 {Black releases the tension in order to free e5 for the c6-knight. We'll regain the pawn and centralize our knight by capturing on d4.} 6.Nxd4 6...Be7 {Black blocks the e-file and prepares to castle. The e4-pawn is now threatened, since Black can easily escape White's threats along the e-file.} 7.Re1 {White brings the rook out and protects the pawn on e4. In the next few moves, White will finish developing their queenside and connect the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {Black takes the free pawn and hopes to get to play d5 on the next move in order to close the diagonal for our bishop and protect their king in the center. We'll take on e5, opening up the d-file for the queen. The d5-square is very attractive, since the queen can threaten both f7 and e4 at the same time from there.} 5.dxe5 5...Bc5 {Black develops the bishop and attacks f2, hoping to gain some time to castle. But we'll ignore the threat on f2 and threatens something even bigger - a checkmate on f7. Also, the knight on e4 is now hanging.} 6.Qd5 6...Bxf2+ {Black takes with check and avoids losing material at the moment.} 7.Ke2 {But after we move our king Black has no further way to attack. Now Black will have to either lose the knight or get checkmated.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d6 {Black fortifies the center and opens up the bishop. A small downside of the move is that the dark-squared bishop is blocked. We'll castle our king into safety.} 5.O-O 5...Nxe4 {Black is greedy and captures a "free" pawn at the cost of their development. We'll fork the two knights, intending to undermine Black's center by trading on c6.} 6.Bd5 6...Nf6 {Black pulls the loose knight back. We'll continue our idea by capturing on c6 and undermining the e-pawn.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black has to recapture. Otherwise, they have lost a whole piece.} 8.dxe5 {White gains the sacrificed material back and opens up the central files that should give them access to the Black king.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d5 {Black counterstrikes in the center and opens up their light-squared bishop. We'll capture on d5, eliminating the attack on our bishop.} 5.exd5 5...Nxd5 {Black recaptures the pawn and centralizes the knight. We'll castle into safety before going on the attack.} 6.O-O 6...exd4 {Black is greedy and grabs pawns instead of focusing on quickly developing their pieces and castling.} 7.Ng5 {White immediately puts pressure on f7 and intends to harass it more by bringing the queen to f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d5 {Black counterstrikes in the center and opens up their light-squared bishop. We'll capture on d5, eliminating the attack on our bishop.} 5.exd5 5...Nxd4 {Black captures on d4 and moves the knight from c6, where it was under attack.} 6.Nxe5 {White exploits Black's last move and grabs an important central pawn. Now Black has to spend time on getting their knight into safety, which should give White time to castle and start attacking black's king.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {Black takes the free pawn and hopes to get to play d5 on the next move in order to close the diagonal for our bishop and protect their king in the center. We'll take on e5, opening up the d-file for the queen. The d5-square is very attractive, since the queen can threaten both f7 and e4 at the same time from there.} 5.dxe5 5...d5 {Black opens up the bishop and protects the knight. We'll just take on d5, reviving the threat to the knight.} 6.Bxd5 6...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop and protects the knight on e4. Let's capture on c6 and disrupt Black's pawns.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black has to recapture. Otherwise, they have lost a whole piece. We'll move the queen off the d-file in order to avoid trading queens.} 8.Qe2 8...Bc5 {Black develops the final minor piece and gets ready to castle.} 9.O-O {We castle the king into safety and will probably continue by developing the queenside and then bringing the rooks to the d- and e-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d6 {Black fortifies the center and opens up the bishop. A small downside of the move is that the dark-squared bishop is blocked. We'll castle our king into safety.} 5.O-O 5...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop and pins the knight. The d4-pawn is now hanging. We'll solve that problem by capturing on e5.} 6.dxe5 6...Nxe5 {Black recaptures and threatens both our bishop and our knight on f3. We'll retreat the bishop and add protection to the knight on f3. This secures our kingside pawn structure.} 7.Be2 7...Nxe4 {Black captures a pawn at the cost of their development. We'll force trades in the center, leading to a sequence which wins material.} 8.Nxe5 8...Bxe2 {If Black does not make this intermediate move, they lose a piece immediately.} 9.Qxe2 {White recaptures with the queen and still wins a piece. If Black moves their knight, White will be able to make a nasty discovered check, and if Black takes on e5, then the knight on e4 falls.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d6 {Black fortifies the center and opens up the bishop. A small downside of the move is that the dark-squared bishop is blocked. We'll castle our king into safety.} 5.O-O 5...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop and pins the knight. The d4-pawn is now hanging. We'll solve that problem by capturing on e5.} 6.dxe5 6...Nxe5 {Black recaptures and threatens both our bishop and our knight on f3. We'll retreat the bishop and add protection to the knight on f3. This secures our kingside pawn structure.} 7.Be2 7...Nxf3+ {Trading in order not to lose material is a better option for Black. We'll recapture with the bishop, maintaining the integrity of our pawn structure.} 8.Bxf3 8...Bxf3 {Black does not want to retreat the bishop and waste more time, so they decide to trade it too.} 9.Qxf3 {White recaptures the piece and develops the queen. In the next few moves, White will most likely develop the queenside and bring the rooks out.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Two Knights Double Gambit / Study 2: Black's Other 4th Moves / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/063af611-2444-4a60-9b9c-88823aa08b3f/chapters/c59b3932-57e1-4606-879a-2b28a77959e4/studies/f4b8e56f-2229-4d8a-92d6-5a45aeaab94e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {The most popular reply for Black. With our response of Nf3, we are developing a piece, controlling the center, attacking the e5 pawn and are one move closer to castling.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black responds by developing their Knight and protecting the pawn. With Bc4, we reach one of the most famous openings in chess - the Italian Game.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black responds with the Two Knights Defense. Now we'll enter our gambit line by playing d4, gaining space in the center and forcing Black to react, plus opening up our dark-squared bishop.} 4.d4 4...d6 {Black fortifies the center and opens up the bishop. A small downside of the move is that the dark-squared bishop is blocked. We'll castle our king into safety.} 5.O-O 5...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop and pins the knight. The d4-pawn is now hanging. We'll solve that problem by capturing on e5.} 6.dxe5 6...dxe5 {Black recaptures the pawn and offers a trade of queens in order to release the pressure. We'll decline the queen trade and develop the knight, protecting e4 and f3.} 7.Nbd2 7...Nd4 {Black takes advantage of the pin in order to centralize their knight. But this is a mistake. We can win two pawns with a little combination. We sacrifice the bishop on f7, setting up a knight fork!} 8.Bxf7+ 8...Kxf7 {Black's best option is to just go along with White's temporary sacrifice.} 9.Nxe5+ {White unpins the knight with a check and will capture the bishop on the next move. With two extra pawns and Black's king not being able to castle, White should win the game without much effort.} *